hunting licenses there we also sold  a lot of guns especially shotguns  and rifles sometimes pistols though too  and knives of course people are always  buying knives and one thing that i  remember about selling all these guns  was you could not sell guns  and ammo  to the same customer  in the same purchase they had to be  purchased separately and you wouldn't  allow them to have the gun and the ammo  at the same time if somebody was buying  both of them then somebody else in  sporting goods in automotive had to  either take the gun or take the ammo and  walk it to the door along with the  person  and then you know once you got to the  door and they went out of the door you'd  give them the ammo so obviously if they  wanted to shoot the place up they they  could have done it quite easily but you  know at least you were like covering  your bets i suppose  and uh and that's that's what i did for  you know  about another three three and a half  weeks or so  and i was starting to get the hang of it  you know it wasn't too bad i was getting  to know my co-workers and you know  settling into a kind of routine and then  i got a call  from  lakeland college  and it was quite interesting they said  hey um  we are getting rid of our  our security guards that we've got and  we're going to start a whole new  security guard thing we've got somebody  to run it we just need a bunch of  officers your ex military would you be  interested in doing this sort of thing  and i was like  uh yeah that sounds pretty good and so  you know we had to do some training  which was fine and they gave us uniforms  and then we had um you know our shifts  and i started working second shift which  was kind of nice because it's it's you  know cool and dark and you know you just  walk around and i'd be on till like five  in the morning or so  and then um  they made me second shift supervisor so  now i'm like you know the the second in  command from this other guy and you had  keys that would get you everywhere on  campus you could do stuff like if you  wanted to make yourself a little meal  you'd go into the cafeteria and open the  place up and get yourself some cereal or  make yourself a sandwich and it was kind  of cool to walk around the campus and  just to see all of the different things  from different perspectives like for  example at the uh the mathematics and  science building where i i did a lot of  my classes there were all these lights  outside and so because of that there are  all these massive spider webs because  the spiders would have you know insects  flying into them and they'd get giant  just huge you know like their bellies  like that fat and you you watch these  spiders and you know walk out by the  little lake that they had and  go through the different steam tunnels  underground and you know it was it was  pretty cool there was also a lot of  boring  time involved in that and uh you know  sometimes there'd be things happening on  campus and you'd have to write people up  or intervene or stuff like that but it  was a it was a pretty decent job overall  and it didn't suck like these other  three factory tree cutting and walmart  job did so that's how i basically ended  that summer and you know kept my fiance  and her family off my back because i was  gainfully employed  i think they probably actually thought  it was like better than the other jobs  or something like that  and uh that's you know that that was  that summer i did a lot of working i've  you know more or less worked every  summer i think  uh except for the one um where i went to  you know come back into to europe in  between uh undergraduate and grad school  um when i was working here in milwaukee  for a year but that's a whole different  story so that is the summer of four jobs  maybe you find it interesting  but it certainly was a  lot of work on my part  over that time and i you know it was not  the happiest of times but i do enjoy  working and i can usually find something  at any given job that's  you know interesting or enjoyable so  that's the whole story  you
 

